WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.462
Aliases and grain solve two different kinds of confusion.

00:00:03.462 --> 00:00:06.417
Aliases make the SQL easier to read.

00:00:06.417 --> 00:00:14.652
If both patients and admissions contain a patient ID column, writing p dot patient ID and a dot patient ID

00:00:14.652 --> 00:00:22.257
makes the source explicit. As queries grow to three, four, or five tables, this becomes very important.

00:00:22.257 --> 00:00:27.397
Once you assign an alias to a table, use that alias consistently.

00:00:27.397 --> 00:00:29.707
Grain helps us interpret the result.

00:00:29.707 --> 00:00:32.312
Suppose a patient has two admissions.

00:00:32.312 --> 00:00:37.782
After joining patients to admissions, the patient name may appear twice.

00:00:37.782 --> 00:00:42.812
That does not automatically mean the data was duplicated incorrectly.

00:00:42.812 --> 00:00:50.562
If one result row represents one admission, then repeating the patient information is expected.

00:00:50.562 --> 00:00:56.067
A common beginner mistake is seeing repeated values and immediately adding DISTINCT.

00:00:56.067 --> 00:01:00.447
That can hide a real modeling problem or remove valid events.

00:01:00.447 --> 00:01:07.787
Before using DISTINCT, ask what each row represents and whether those rows correspond to different real

00:01:07.787 --> 00:01:12.547
events. A good habit is to write a short grain sentence before a complicated query.

00:01:12.547 --> 00:01:20.409
For this example, the sentence is: one output row represents one admission with patient information attached.
